  annotation "note_1771583048937_0", anchor: "knowledge written in ciphers only three living penguins can read", text: "The epistemological confidence required to count the readers of an unreadable cipher. If only three penguins can read it, who conducted the census? Either a fourth once could and is now dead — in which case \"three living\" is doing careful grammatical work — or ☽'tenebris herself supplied the number, which means it is marketing.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771583048937_1", anchor: "sold the resulting blank stone to those who wanted their pasts erased", text: "So here she is. In my Shawl☽ Sun annotation I asked how many dead sites I catalogued as natural ice-memory loss were in fact purchased erasures. ☽'tenebris was selling them commercially during the Clever Ice Age. The blank patches in the deep archive that I attributed to corruption damage, to geological compression, to time — some percentage of my life's work has been cataloguing her invoices as geology. I find this funnier than I should.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771583048937_2", anchor: "forty-three aurora-shards per Class II unit", text: "Not forty. Not fifty. Forty-three. My documented hostility toward round numbers obliges me to note, with something approaching delight, that this is precisely the kind of figure that emerges from actual negotiation rather than retrospective estimation. Someone haggled this into existence. I trust it more than any number in the casualty records.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771583048937_3", anchor: "No unit bearing the mark has been returned", text: "An irresistible sales claim. Also unfalsifiable — a returned unit would presumably be returned to her, and she keeps ledgers in memory, and her memory is compromised by the very magic that creates the mark. The guarantee and its audit trail consume each other. Nightshelf commerce at its finest.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
